\mathbf{y} | \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{A}) P(\mathbf{x}) \). To perform inference efficiently in high-dimensional settings, we utilize message-passing algorithms derived from belief propagation (BP). BP approximates the marginal posterior distributions through iterative updates that leverage the factorized structure of the posterior, as defined on a factor graph representation of the problem [1]. This approach ensures computational tractability while maintaining high accuracy in the large-system limit. The theoretical analysis of the algorithms is conducted using the replica method, a non-rigorous yet powerful technique from statistical physics. The replica method provides asymptotic predictions for the behavior of the inference problem by analyzing the free energy landscape of the corresponding statistical model [2, 3]. This analysis identifies phase transitions between regions where inference is easy, hard, or impossible. #### Belief Propagation for Linear and Bilinear Systems For linear inference problems, where \( \mathbf{y} = \mathbf{A} \mathbf{x} +  oldsymbol{\eta} \), with \(  oldsymbol{\eta} \) representing additive noise, the posterior \( P(\mathbf{x} | \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{A}) \) is defined by the Gaussian likelihood \( P(\mathbf{y} | \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{A}) \propto \exp(-\| \mathbf{y} - \mathbf{A} \mathbf{x} \|^2 / 2\sigma^2) \). The BP algorithm operates by iteratively exchanging messages between variable nodes \( x_i \) and factor nodes corresponding to the likelihood terms \( P(y_\mu | \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{A}) \). The messages are updated based on the cavity method [4]: \[ m_{i 	o \mu}(x_i) \propto P(x_i) \prod_{
