Statistical inference is a fundamental component of a wide range of scientific and engineering applications, including signal processing, imaging, machine learning, and communications. Many of these problems can be broadly categorized within a unifying framework that involves recovering structured signals from indirect and often noisy measurements. The interplay between high-dimensional statistics, linear transforms, and nonlinear probabilistic sensing has been a vibrant area of research over the last few decades, leading to significant theoretical insights and the development of efficient algorithms. This research focuses on the statistical physics of such inference problems, encompassing both linear and bilinear settings, and explores their associated challenges, phase transitions, and algorithmic solutions. The classical problem of linear inference, exemplified by compressed sensing, has been extensively studied in recent years. Compressed sensing introduced a paradigm shift by demonstrating that sparse signals can be recovered from a number of linear measurements far below the ambient signal dimension, provided that specific structural properties of the sensing matrix are satisfied [1, 2]. This breakthrough has had a profound impact on fields such as medical imaging, radar signal processing, and wireless communications. The theoretical understanding of compressed sensing has been enriched by tools from high-dimensional probability and statistical physics, particularly the replica method and belief propagation algorithms [3, 4]. These tools have shed light on the fundamental limits of recovery, the structure of the solution space, and the computational complexity of associated algorithms. Generalized linear models extend beyond the scope of standard compressed sensing by incorporating a nonlinear measurement function, which maps the linear mixture of signal components to observed data. This generalization is motivated by numerous practical applications, including logistic regression in machine learning, signal recovery in non-Gaussian noise environments, and phase retrieval in optical imaging systems [5]. In such settings, the nonlinearity introduces additional challenges, as it renders the posterior distribution of the signal highly non-Gaussian. Consequently, efficient algorithms for inference must grapple with this added complexity, often requiring advanced approximation techniques. Bilinear inference problems, which form the focus of a more recent wave of research, involve recovering two sets of coupled variables from measurements that depend bilinearly on these unknowns. Examples include blind source separation, matrix factorization, and matrix completion, all of which have applications in diverse domains such as collaborative filtering, recommendation systems, and genomics [6, 7]. In bilinear problems, the coupling between unknown variables induces a non-convex optimization landscape, characterized by multiple local minima and saddle points. Understanding this landscape is crucial for determining when efficient recovery is feasible and when computational hardness emerges. The Bayesian framework provides a principled approach to inference in high-dimensional problems, as it incorporates prior knowledge about the signal structure and systematically accounts for uncertainty. However, computing the posterior distribution in high dimensions is computationally demanding, due to the curse of dimensionality. Belief propagation, a message-passing algorithm rooted in graphical models, has proven to be a powerful tool for approximate Bayesian inference [8]. By leveraging the sparse interactions in factorized distributions, belief propagation reduces the computational burden, enabling efficient sampling and marginalization even for large-scale problems. Statistical physics offers a complementary perspective on high-dimensional inference. Concepts such as energy landscapes, free energy, and phase transitions, originally developed 